Usage: hacs --bench <mode> <args...> [--repeat k] [--cross-checks]

Modes:
  cohom  <n_sym> <ell>            EquivariantCohomologyOnK on the SL(2,Z) model, all q
  hecke  <n_sym> <ell> <p>        T_p end to end (cosets -> tau -> Theta_g -> A^(q))
  family <n_sym> <ell> <p> <r>    T_{p^r} by recursion AND by the direct double-coset route
  decomp <n_sym> <ell> <p>        + primary module decomposition of the largest H^q
  ext    <n_sym> <ell> [p,q,...]  + the extension layer, Stages 1-5 (default family 2,3,5)
  micro  <op> <args...>           Phase-2 arithmetic micro-benchmarks; `micro --help`
                                  lists the ops (gemm/gemv/rref/kernel/transpose/suite)

Options:
  --repeat k          run the whole pipeline k times; print min/median of the totals
  --backend b         (mode `micro`) legacy | engine; default engine
                      (`legacy` = the pre-Phase-2 substrate; ENGINE_BACKEND
                      sets the default)
  --cross-checks      (mode `hecke`) additionally run the alpha/beta/omega route
                      (off by default: it roughly doubles the measured time)
  --lean              memory-lean configuration: drop the cohomology objects no
                      production consumer reads and skip the Theta_eq certification
                      (A^(q) unchanged; reported as lean=1 on the BENCH line)
  --count-allocs      report heap allocations (count and requested bytes) made
                      during each measured repeat, via a counting replacement
                      global operator new; adds allocs=/alloc_bytes= to the
                      BENCH line. With -DENGINE_COUNT_OBJECTS=ON it also reports
                      FlatMatrix / BlockFlatMatrix constructions.

Every run ends with one machine-readable line:
  BENCH mode=... n=... ell=... [p=...] [r=...] total_s=... rss_mb=... <mode-specific keys>
(rss_mb is peak RSS over the whole process, from /proc/self/status VmHWM.)

NOTE: on this model H^*(K, R; Sym^n) vanishes for ODD n (the -I / odd-n base-model
gap); use an even n_sym for a meaningful measurement.
